Summary: D3DMetal and DXMT: export winemac's function table for Metal views
Category: graphics
Kind: feature
Origin: crossover (ported here)
  Version: 26.3.0
  CW-Hacks: 22435
  URL: https://media.codeweavers.com/pub/crossover/source/crossover-sources-26.3.0.tar.gz
  Files: dlls/winemac.drv/d3dmetal.c, dlls/winemac.drv/d3dmetal_objc.h,
    dlls/winemac.drv/d3dmetal_objc.m, dlls/winemac.drv/cocoa_window.m,
    dlls/winemac.drv/event.c, dlls/winemac.drv/window.c,
    dlls/winemac.drv/macdrv.h, dlls/winemac.drv/macdrv_cocoa.h,
    dlls/winemac.drv/Makefile.in
  Changes: CX 26 (Wine 11.0) reworked the hack for Wine 11's client
    surfaces: get_win_data() makes a client surface per DXGI swapchain and
    hands the renderer its view; a WineMetalLayer subclass posts
    CLIENT_SURFACE_PRESENTED from nextDrawable so win32u's
    client_surface_present() runs. Adapted to WineHQ 11.21: Objective-C types
    (WineWindow *, WineContentView *, WineMetalView *, MTLDevice_id,
    CAMetalLayer *) instead of the macdrv_* opaque typedefs; the surface comes
    from macdrv_CreateClientSurface(hwnd, 0, FALSE); DestroyWindow releases
    the surfaces after dropping the win_data lock (releasing takes win32u's
    surfaces lock, which present takes before win_data). Left out, as only
    D3DMetal uses them: CX's registry callbacks (dllmain.c, macdrv_main.c,
    unixlib.h, the advapi32 delay import) and user32 entries; the table ends
    after on_main_thread and the window data after client_cocoa_view (DXMT's
    own declarations of both stop there). Added: nextDrawable returns at once
    until a view has been given a client surface, and a failed calloc in
    get_win_data returns NULL instead of crashing.
  Note: the wine.inf part of CW HACK 22435 is
    wine-inf-d3dmetal-placeholder-dlls. Sikarugir 10.0_8 ships the Wine 10.0
    form: winemac.so exports _macdrv_functions; trace strings
    my_macdrv_view_create_metal_view, "macdrv_view_create_metal_view %p %p",
    my_RegQueryValueExA … my_SetWindowLongPtrW, debug channel macdrv_d3dmtl;
    winemac.drv contains macdrv_regqueryvalueexa and delay-imports
    advapi32.dll
Upstream: no
Why: DXMT draws into Wine's windows through winemac: it looks up the table
 winemac.so exports as macdrv_functions (with dlsym) to get a window's client
 view, make a Metal view and layer in it, and release them (DXMT v0.74 calls
 get_win_data, release_win_data and the three metal_view functions). In Wine
 11, each such swapchain gets a win32u client surface, and the Metal layer
 reports each frame (CLIENT_SURFACE_PRESENTED) so win32u composites it. Every
 Metal view's layer, MoltenVK's included, is a WineMetalLayer; until a
 renderer has asked for a client surface, its nextDrawable returns at once.
 D3DMetal uses the same table, with registry and user32 entries after these
 and a larger window data structure; this copy leaves those out, as this
 build doesn't support D3DMetal on Wine 11.x.
Programs: none known
Enable: see ntdll-renderer-dll-paths (WINEDLLPATH_DXMT)
Messages: TRACE lines on the macdrv_d3dmtl channel, such as "get_win_data %p"
 and "macdrv_view_create_metal_view %p %p" (hidden unless WINEDEBUG has
 +macdrv_d3dmtl)
Risk: exposes winemac internals to any dylib in the process, by design
Uncertain: the Wine 11 adaptation hasn't run with DXMT (it needs a GUI
 session). Client surfaces made per get_win_data call stay in the window's
 list until the window is destroyed, so a program that recreates its
 swapchain often collects them. Once any view has a client surface, every
 Metal layer's nextDrawable reads its view's superview and window on the
 renderer's thread, as CX's does; AppKit doesn't document those getters as
 thread-safe.
Status: 11.22: 2026-11-26: D3DMetal-only parts removed, the per-frame check
 skipped until a view is marked, calloc checked
 11.22: 2026-11-26: quick builds 20261126T002454 and T025644 (macOS 26.7.2,
 M1 Max): smoke and the regression suite passed
 11.22: 2026-11-26: DXMT v0.74 through WINEDLLPATH_DXMT on quick T025644, in a
 Screen Slots login (macOS 26.7.2, M1 Max): a Direct3D 11 program loaded
 DXMT's d3d11.dll from system32, got the M1 Max at feature level 11_0, and
 presented about 2,400 frames of the green it cleared to, as on screen

